  "branch": "inorganic",
  "b6_answer": "4",
  "b6_reason": "Dilute sulphuric acid is added because it suppresses Fe2+ hydrolysis without oxidising Fe2+ and introduces no foreign anion into the sulphate salt.",
  "b6_working": "Mohr's salt is FeSO4.(NH4)2SO4.6H2O. Fe2+ + 2H2O -> Fe(OH)2 + 2H+ is suppressed by added H+. HNO3 (dilute or not) would oxidise Fe2+ to Fe3+; conc. H2SO4 is also oxidising and dehydrating; HCl adds chloride, a foreign ion. Dilute H2SO4 is the correct choice.",
